    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form across the stack. The test runtime uses HSQLDB, and both acceptance tests (structured normalization/composition and flat backward-compatibility) are green.\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` (structured, preferred) and optional `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (same `type/minLength/maxLength/pattern` as `address`), documented `address` as the composed/backward-compatible field, and removed `address` from the `required` list. Removing it from `required` is essential: bean validation runs on the request body, so a structured owner (which omits `address`) would otherwise be rejected with 400 before reaching the controller. `city` stays required.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields (mapped to `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns) with getters/setters. MapStruct picks these up automatically for both request mapping and the response DTO, so the response now exposes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` alongside the composed `address`.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Rewrote `normalizeAddressFields` to prefer the structured form: when a non-blank `addressLine1` is supplied it (and any `addressLine2`) is normalized, and the flat `address` is composed as normalized `addressLine1` + a single space + normalized `addressLine2` (only when `addressLine2` is present). Otherwise it falls back to normalizing the flat `address`. An owner is rejected (400) only when neither form yields a non-blank address. Because the composed value is written back to `address`, everything downstream that reads the address sees the structured value with a flat fallback.\n\n4. **DB schemas** (`db/h2`, `db/hsqldb`, `db/mysql`, `db/postgres`) \u2014 Added nullable `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to the `owners` table. The flat `address` column stays `NOT NULL` and is always populated by the controller's composition, so structured owners persist cleanly.\n\nNormalization (trim, whitespace-collapse, upper-case, `ST`\u2192`STREET` etc.) reuses the existing `Owner.normalizeAddress`, so structured and flat inputs canonicalize identically \u2014 e.g. `\"10 king st\"` \u2192 `addressLine1` and `address` both `\"10 KING STREET\"`.",
